# Analysis II proof-debt ledger.
#
# This file is comment-only and is not exported by litex.config.
#
# Chapter 1 currently has 26 explicit trust statements. The ordered project
# runner succeeds, so these are mathematics-completeness boundaries rather
# than runner failures.
#
# 1.1.18: prove the four finite-dimensional l1/l2/linf/coordinate convergence
# directions. Also replace the trusted unique-maximum construction used for
# linf_distance after the recursive-template kernel problem is fixed.
# After 1.1.20: expose the selected metric-limit notation once dependent
# unique selection propagates its certificate through template instantiation.
# 1.2.10: construct a sequence by choosing a point from each 1/n ball; the
# other three equivalence directions are checkable.
# 1.2.15: prove the remaining open-ball, family, interior-openness, and
# closure-closedness properties. The closed-ball and singleton-closed
# directions, both complement directions, plus the maximal-open-subset and
# smallest-closed-superset inclusions, are checked.
# 1.3.4: formalize the source's relative-open union construction and the
# source-deferred closed-set half.
# 1.4.5: construct a convergent subsequence by choosing a point in each 1/n
# ball; the converse is checked. 1.4.12 retains the completeness and
# closed-subspace proof debt; Lemma 1.4.9 is checked.
# 1.5.5-1.5.10: prove the remaining compactness consequences, the reverse
# finite-dimensional Heine--Borel direction, the open-cover theorem, nested
# compact intersection, and finite-union/finite-set compactness. The forward
# Heine--Borel direction and compact-subset closedness are checked derivations
# from Corollary 1.5.6 and therefore retain its indirect trust boundary.
#
# Chapter 2, Section 2.1 — Theorem 2.1.4
# The three continuity characterizations are stated through four reusable
# directions. The forward epsilon--delta sequence-limit proof is checked.
# The shrinking-ball countersequence and open-neighborhood directions remain.
#
# Chapter 2, Section 2.1 — Theorem 2.1.5
# The sequential characterization is established from Theorem 2.1.4. The
# remaining gap is proving the four directions relating continuity to open
# and closed inverse images.
#
# Chapter 2, Section 2.2 — Lemmas 2.2.1 and 2.2.2
# The paired function and all seven arithmetic maps are concrete. The missing
# mathematics is the coordinatewise convergence proof for pairing and the
# real limit-law proofs of continuity for the arithmetic maps.
#
# Chapter 2, Section 2.2 — Corollary 2.2.3
# All global arithmetic-combination conclusions are established from their
# pointwise forms. The remaining gaps are the seven pointwise composition
# identifications, including the restricted-codomain argument for division.
#
# Chapter 2, Section 2.3 — Theorem 2.3.1 and Proposition 2.3.2
# Prove compactness of continuous images and then the boundedness and attained
# extrema of continuous real functions on compact spaces.
#
# Chapter 2, Section 2.3 — Theorem 2.3.5
# Uniform continuity is proved to imply continuity. The remaining direction
# is the finite-open-cover and finite-minimum argument on a compact domain.
#
# Chapter 2, Section 2.4 — Theorems 2.4.5-2.4.7
# Supply the supremum proof characterizing connected real subsets, the
# pullback-of-separation proof for continuous images, and the resulting
# intermediate value argument.
#
# Chapter 2, Section 2.5
# All eight named topological definitions are concrete and checked. The
# source-deferred exercises proving induced-topology laws and equivalence with
# the metric notions are excluded by the inventory policy and add no trust.
#
# Chapter 3
# 3.1.5: prove the sequential, neighborhood, and extension equivalences. The
# value-at-domain-point consequence is checked directly.
# 3.3.1-3.3.6: formalize the epsilon/3 uniform-limit arguments and boundedness.
# Corollary 3.3.2 is checked from the pointwise theorem.
# 3.4.2-3.4.5: expose a reusable supremum selector, prove the metric
# convergence equivalence, and complete the bounded-continuous-space proof.
# 3.5.7: prove the M-test through uniform Cauchy completeness.
# 3.6.1-3.7.3: connect concrete Analysis I integral and derivative APIs and
# prove the interchange theorems and their series corollaries.
# 3.8.3-3.8.19: discharge the support, convolution, zero-extension, and
# polynomial approximation proof chain.
#
# Chapter 4
# 4.1.3-4.1.6: connect an extended-real limsup/radius interface and prove
# convergence, uniform convergence, and termwise calculus.
# 4.2.4-4.2.12: connect the Analysis I derivative API and prove the derivative
# tower, Taylor coefficient formula, analyticity of derivatives, and
# uniqueness of coefficients.
# 4.3.1-4.4.1: prove Abel's endpoint theorem, summation by parts, and the
# Cauchy-product/convolution theorem.
# 4.5.1-4.5.6: prove existence and uniqueness of the exponential/logarithm
# selections and their analytic, derivative, order, product, inverse, integral,
# and series laws. Replace the cross-book real-power graph when a canonical
# Analysis I dependency exists.
# 4.6.4-4.6.15: prove the coordinate algebra, conjugation, norm, inverse,
# metric, coordinate-limit, limit-law, and complex-exponential results.
# Addition and multiplication commutativity are checked. The remaining basic
# coordinate laws expose nested struct-valued function unfolding rather than
# a need for new abstract mathematical interfaces.
# 4.7.1-4.7.5: connect the real restrictions of complex sine and cosine, prove
# the identities and derivatives, establish the least positive sine zero, and
# prove periodicity and the two zero-set characterizations.
#
# Chapter 5
# 5.1.5-5.5.4: supply the analytic proofs for periodic-function closure,
# Hermitian and L2 laws, character orthogonality, coefficient recovery,
# periodic convolution, Fejer approximation, Fourier convergence, absolute
# convergence, and Plancherel. The periodic, character, trigonometric
# polynomial, inner-product, convolution, and convergence relations themselves
# are concrete. Fourier coefficient coordinates, their symmetric partial-sum
# family, the absolute-summability hypothesis, and the Plancherel energy
# sequence are now tied together explicitly; their analytic proofs remain
# trusted.
#
# Chapter 6
# 6.1.13-6.8.1: prove matrix representation, uniqueness and calculus laws for
# total derivatives, continuous-partial and Clairaut theorems, contraction
# mapping, the quantitative invertible-linear-map estimate, and the inverse
# and implicit function theorems. Finite coordinate spaces, linearity,
# matrices, derivatives, contractions, fixed points, and the hypotheses and
# conclusions of the small-identity-perturbation lemma are concrete. Replace
# the remaining analytic proof debts in the local neighborhood, inverse-law,
# inverse-derivative, implicit graph, and implicit-derivative interfaces.
#
# Chapter 7
# 7.1.1-7.5.10: construct Lebesgue measure from the displayed outer-measure
# relation and prove the outer-measure, Caratheodory measurability,
# countable-additivity, Borel, and measurable-function theorems. Open boxes,
# covers, outer-measure values, measurable sets, and measurable functions are
# concrete. The finite and countable nonadditivity counterexamples now state
# their disjoint families, member values, union values, and failed equalities;
# formalize their choice-based constructions and add an extended-nonnegative
# value carrier for the cases involving positive infinity.
#
# Chapter 8
# 8.1.3-8.5.1: prove simple-function algebra and presentation independence,
# the nonnegative integral laws, monotone convergence, Fatou, almost-everywhere
# invariance, signed-integral laws, dominated convergence, Riemann
# compatibility, and Fubini. Simple, nonnegative, signed, upper, and lower
# integral relations are concrete. Tonelli, dominated convergence, and the
# upper/lower common-value criterion now have complete source-facing
# hypotheses and conclusions; only their proof steps remain trusted.
